+## 11. From the Alpha 5 playtest
+
+| What happened | Why | What changes |
+|---|---|---|
+| Only the first journey could be played | the title screen takes the map's first journey | a journey picker on the title card |
+| The plan said horseback; the gold line went by sea | Bernoy's generated journeys send land legs over water: in *The Leg Keels*, 90% and 93% of the foot and horseback legs' lines lie on water *(data)*; so do legs of three other journeys | a leg that cannot go overland becomes "ride, then cross by sea": a passage stop is added, par is recomputed, and the story takes the crossing as a moment |
+| The company rowboat could not be left or sold | the shipwright only deals with owned and hired vessels | the yard takes company vessels: leave her in the harbour's care, sell her, or hand her back to the company |
+| "Ask the company": you had no company on a raid | the company forms only at a Gathering stop, and *The Leg Keels* has none | types with a company form it at the start; the opening names them |
+| You could strike with your crew at the other side of the world | the climax checked nothing | a strike needs the company; without them the Keels strike without you, and the share is theirs |
+| "Look the place over" at Vr'seramant, when the target was Negnud | the beat took the nearest walls as a trial | the leg into the target is its approach; scouting belongs there |
+| Hook text read like data ("where the X gives way to the Y") | the scene showed the hook | moments replace hook text |
+| The journey read as a list of binary choices | each beat was one scene | leg roles give objectives that last a leg; choices come from people and situations along it |
+
+---
+
+## 12. Build order
+
+**Alpha 5.1: the ground under the story.** The journey picker; land legs over water reconciled; the
+company formed at the start; company vessels at the yard; engine words hidden; the premise card and the
+objective in the journal and status line.
+
+**Alpha 5.2: arcs and leg roles.** Arcs, leg-role libraries and structure weights as data; objectives
+that last a leg; purpose on the road (moments from towns near the route); measures shown in the journal;
+endings read from the measure.
+
+**Alpha 5.3: carriers and identity.** Innkeeper, shrine-keeper, elder, captain of the watch and
+townsfolk as helpers and carriers; disposition from people, faith, nationality, state form, the world
+and nature; prices, help, refusal and night robbery from disposition; questioning in mysteries.
+
+**Later.** Alpha 6 carries moments as news and knowledge; Alpha 7 brings raids, inspections and
+suspicion in detail.
+
+---
+
+## 13. Decisions, and what Alpha 5.1 built
+
+1. **Ties** are part of the character, with leading or following: accept the traveller, or roll
+   another.
+2. **Measures** are told in words: five per arc, from nothing to the most.
+3. **Structure weights** ship as in section 6, to be tuned in play.
+4. **Lead or follow** is part of the character.
+5. **Objectives** show in the corner: specific enough to steer by, open enough to choose the way.
+   Asking the right person completes a question and moves the objective on.
+6. **Disposition** is one system with Alpha 7's suspicion: regard. What the narrative needs is built
+   (people, faith, state, form, world, nature, standing, suspicion from your deeds; prices, help,
+   night thieves, the watch); papers, dress, speech, contraband and alert levels wait for Alpha 7.
+
+Waiting for later versions: raids in detail (Alpha 7; a placeholder scene marks each), questioning
+in mysteries, rivals that move, the company's stop calls by its rule, news of moments (Alpha 6), and
+par recomputed for legs that cross the sea.
